.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1202 AM PDT Tue Aug 25 2026
(Today and tonight)

Satellite imagery shows an expanding stratus layer below
scattered high clouds, with low clouds present in the valleys of
southern Sonoma County, the San Francisco Bay region, and down the
Salinas Valley. Coastal drizzle has been observed at the office,
and has been added to the grids tonight. The stratus deck is
expected to continue developing into the SF Bay region through the
night before retreating back to the coastal regions after
sunrise.

After a slight dip in the temperatures yesterday, temperatures will
tick upward today as the balance between troughing in the eastern
Pacific and ridging over the Desert Southwest swings back towards
the ridging influence. Temperatures range from the 80s into the
middle 90s for the inland valleys, up to around 104 in the southern
tier of Monterey County, the upper 60s to the lower 80s near the
Bays, and the upper 50s to middle 60s along the Pacific Coast.
Breezy onshore winds will develop this afternoon and evening, with
wind gusts 20 to 30 mph along the coast and through favored gaps and
passes. The marine layer will remain around 1000-1500 ft today, with
warm and dry conditions above the marine layer inversion. See the
FIRE WEATHER section for the implications for local fire weather
concerns.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1202 AM PDT Tue Aug 25 2026
(Wednesday through Monday)

The ridging prevails into Thursday with temperatures in the middle
80s to the middle 90s inland and the 70s into the middle 80s near
the Bays. A pattern change begins on Friday with upper level
troughing developing over the West Coast, bringing cooler
temperatures and breezier winds to the region. Temperatures in the
inland valleys dip into the 80s for the weekend, and cool further
with highs dipping into the middle 70s to lower 80s early next week.
Even the warm spots of the interior Central Coast cool into the
middle to upper 80s by Monday. The strengthening pressure gradient
will allow stronger onshore flow to develop starting on Friday, with
wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph expected along the coast, through favored
gaps and passes, and within the northern Salinas Valley.

We continue to monitor an active tropical Eastern Pacific and its
indirect impacts to the region. Tropical Storm Iselle continues to
march northwestward off the coast of western Mexico, and a Tropical
Depression has spun up for around a day or so before it gets
absorbed into Iselle. In addition, our colleagues at the National
Hurricane Center are tracking three potential development areas
through the next week. None of these storms are expected to directly
impact the state, but long-period swell generated by the intense
winds within the storms is expected to begin arriving Friday into
the weekend. The current forecast remains just below Beach Hazards
Statement criteria, but this will get refined through the next
couple of days. There`s also an increasing chance that tropical
moisture will impact the region during Friday and the weekend, with
the model trend being to push the moisture plume farther up the
coast into the Bay Area and Central Coast. The NBM continues to show
a very low (below 5% probability), but non-zero, chance for
thunderstorms during this period, and given the uncertainties
surrounding eastern Pacific tropical weather, this setup bears
continued monitoring.

