.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1202 AM PDT Tue Aug 25 2026

Near-critical fire weather conditions continue through Thursday for
elevations above 1000 feet as warm and dry conditions continue
across the region. Humidity recoveries remain poor to very poor in
the higher elevations, with nocturnal drying expected within the
Santa Lucia range. Breezy conditions will develop each afternoon and
evening, but not for a long enough period to trigger any fire
weather products. Specifically regarding the Timber Fire vicinity,
observational data suggests that southwesterly winds from the
Pacific Ocean over the ridgetops are helping to moisten the air,
with nocturnal drying being observed as the winds diminish and even
turn faintly offshore overnight. This has led to an unusual
situation where daily maximum relative humidity values have been
observed during the daytime. Smoke transport from the Timber Fire
will continue to peak in the afternoon and evening with the plume
spreading towards the Salinas area. Friday into the weekend, a
pattern change should allow for some relief as an upper level trough
comes into the region bringing cooler temperatures and a deeper
marine layer.

